Fix upstream bool-override parsing in check_and_refine_config (RFdiffusion
86507b6538f51fce57b5a72477165f03999ed7ae).

The checkpoint config is copied over the hydra overrides and then the
overrides are re-applied with `mytype(value_string)`. For booleans that
means bool("false") which is True, so `preprocess.sidechain_input=false`
silently becomes True for the binder checkpoint (trained with
sidechain_input=True) — and that code path is broken upstream (seq == 21
on a one-hot (L,22) tensor raises "too many indices for tensor of
dimension 3"). Parse boolean strings explicitly so the override lands and
the well-tested sidechain-free path runs.

--- a/rfdiffusion/inference/model_runners.py
+++ b/rfdiffusion/inference/model_runners.py
@@ -240,14 +240,15 @@
                 print(
                     f'WARNING: You are changing {override.split("=")[0]} from the value this model was trained with. Are you sure you know what you are doing?'
                 )
-                mytype = type(
-                    self._conf[override.split(".")[0]][
-                        override.split(".")[1].split("=")[0]
-                    ]
-                )
-                self._conf[override.split(".")[0]][
-                    override.split(".")[1].split("=")[0]
-                ] = mytype(override.split("=")[1])
+                key = override.split(".")[1].split("=")[0]
+                value = override.split("=")[1]
+                mytype = type(self._conf[override.split(".")[0]][key])
+                if mytype is bool:
+                    # bool("false") is True in Python — parse boolean strings explicitly.
+                    parsed = value.lower() == "true"
+                else:
+                    parsed = mytype(value)
+                self._conf[override.split(".")[0]][key] = parsed

     def load_model(self):
         """Create RosettaFold model from preloaded checkpoint."""
